WEBVTT

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:13.023
Compost is what happens when microbes eat manure, straw, food and garden waste.

00:00:13.173 --> 00:00:19.633
Bacteria and fungi break down carbon-rich browns and nitrogen-rich greens, ideally mixed about 25–35 : 1.

00:00:19.783 --> 00:00:25.550
With oxygen, they turn it into CO₂, water, heat and humus, the dark, stable part of good soil.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.605
Microbes can work with oxygen (aerobic) or without it (anaerobic). The difference matters.

00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:38.274
Aerobic: fast, hot (55–70 °C) and almost odourless. Carbon leaves as CO₂ and most nitrogen stays in the compost.

00:00:38.424 --> 00:00:44.550
Anaerobic: slow and cool. A wet, airless pile gives off methane, about 28× as warming as CO₂, and loses nitrogen as ammonia.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.690
Microbes need four things: the right food mix, water, air, and a pile big enough to stay warm.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:01:00.850
Water: 50–60 %, as damp as a wrung-out sponge. Too dry and they stall. Too wet and water fills the air gaps, so the pile goes anaerobic.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:09.550
Air: at least 5 % oxygen in the gaps, so turn the pile or blow air through it. And at least about 1 m³, or it loses heat faster than it makes it.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.533
As the microbes feed, they multiply, and their metabolism releases heat.

00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:21.825
Every kilogram of organic matter they break down releases about 18 MJ, roughly 5 kWh.

00:01:21.975 --> 00:01:28.550
The pile insulates itself, so heat builds up faster than it escapes: from 15 °C to over 60 °C in a few days.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.271
That rise and fall gives the four phases of composting.

00:01:34.421 --> 00:01:43.409
Mesophilic, 20–45 °C, for the first days. Then thermophilic, 45–70 °C: heat-loving microbes take over, and 3 days above 55 °C kill pathogens and weed seeds.

00:01:43.559 --> 00:01:51.550
Cooling and maturation: fungi and slower microbes finish the job over weeks to months. In an open pile, each turning brings oxygen back in.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.467
The project started with Compostito, a home composter with one chamber per phase and a water coil in the hot one.

00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:07.622
But compost passes heat on poorly: 99.5 % of the resistance is on the compost side, so a buried pipe collects only about 50 W.

00:02:07.772 --> 00:02:14.550
And a box that small loses its heat through the walls. At home the maths didn't add up, so the design moved to farm scale.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.760
On a farm, the same process runs in four insulated tunnels. Each takes a 147 t batch of manure, silage and straw for 15 days.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:32.717
Fans blow air up through a slotted floor, and a controller holds the bed at 60 °C: about 4 days mesophilic, then 11 thermophilic.

00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:40.550
72 hours above 55 °C sanitise every batch. It cools to 45 °C, then matures for 8 weeks in windrows, where no heat is collected.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:50.739
The exhaust leaves the bed saturated at 60 °C. 92 % of its heat is in the water vapour, so the exchanger condenses it.

00:02:50.889 --> 00:02:58.297
That heats water to 50 °C for dairy hot water, barns and greenhouses: about 2 GWh a year from 14,279 t of feedstock.

00:02:58.447 --> 00:03:07.550
Taking that heat dries the bed: about 63 t of water leave each batch, and 44 t condense back. Spray bars rewet the bed if it falls below 52 %.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.046
After 8 weeks of maturation, what comes out of the windrow is dark, crumbly compost.

00:03:15.196 --> 00:03:23.550
Because it was made with oxygen, it is stable and sanitised, holds its nutrients, and adds organic matter that keeps water and feeds soil life.
